---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleFaq from '../../../components/ArticleFaq.astro';
import CTASection from '../../../components/CTASection.astro';
import { MANUFACTURER, CERTIFICATIONS, TRADE_TERMS, RELATIONSHIP } from '../../../data/site';

const title = 'How OEM Battery Programs Work | Dingwei Battery';
const description = 'How an OEM battery program runs end to end — the 7-step flow from consultation to repeat orders, with the deliverable and buyer sign-off at each stage.';
const articleTitle = 'How OEM battery programs work';

const faqs = [
  { q: 'How many stages does an OEM battery program have?', a: '7 stages, from consultation through specification, design, sampling, production, quality inspection and shipping — but the buyer only signs off 4 of them: specification, sample, quality standard and shipment.' },
  { q: 'What makes an OEM program different from a one-off order?', a: 'The specification lock — a program locks the approved sample so the 10th order matches the 1st, while a one-off order has no such guarantee.' },
  { q: 'How long until I reach repeat orders?', a: 'About 60–90 days to the first container, then repeat orders are shorter because the specification is locked and only production time (30–45 days) remains.' },
  { q: 'What is the most important buyer sign-off?', a: 'The sample. Approving a drawing but not testing the physical sample is how a repeat order comes back different from what you approved — and it is the mistake behind most drifting 60–90 day programs.' },
  { q: 'What does a first OEM order cost to set up?', a: 'A 20ft container of roughly 600–800 batteries, plus tooling and certification where the specification needs it — the unit cost is lowest at container scale.' }
];
---
<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} articleTitle={articleTitle} articleSection="OEM" dateModified="2026-09-10" faqs={faqs}>
  <section class="section container prose">
    <span class="tag">Knowledge · OEM</span>
    <h1>How OEM battery programs work</h1>
    <p class="byline">Published by Dingwei Battery · Last reviewed 2026-09-10 · Source: Chengguang Power Tech Co., Ltd. technical documentation</p>
    <p class="lead">An OEM battery program is a repeatable supply arrangement, not a one-off order. The point of the structure is simple: make the tenth container match the first approved sample. That repeatability is what the program buys you — and it is what a loose one-off order cannot.</p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> An OEM battery program runs in 7 stages — consultation, specification, design and branding, sampling, mass production, quality inspection and shipping — but you only sign off 4 of them: specification, sample, quality standard and shipment. The mechanism that makes the whole thing safe is the specification lock: an approved sample plus written parameters plus change control, so repeat orders match the first.
    </div>

    <h2>The seven-stage flow</h2>
    <p>The program follows a fixed sequence, and each stage has one deliverable and one checkpoint. Skipping a stage is how a specification drifts.</p>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Manufacturer delivers</th><th>Buyer action</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1. Consultation</td><td>Feasibility view on the requested spec</td><td>Confirm vehicle, market and volume intent</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2. Specification</td><td>Written spec sheet, every parameter</td><td>Sign every line, including the CCA test basis</td></tr>
        <tr><td>3. Design &amp; branding</td><td>Label, case and packaging artwork</td><td>Approve artwork and documentation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>4. Sampling</td><td>Pre-production sample + test report</td><td>Test the physical sample before approving</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5. Mass production</td><td>Scheduled production, in-process control</td><td>Confirm schedule and any change freeze</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6. Quality inspection</td><td>Inspection and test data</td><td>Agree acceptance criteria before release</td></tr>
        <tr><td>7. Shipping</td><td>Export packaging and documents</td><td>Confirm documents before shipment</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>The specification lock</h2>
    <p>The core of an OEM program is not the first order; it is the locked specification that makes the tenth order match the first approved sample. The lock has three parts:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The approved sample</strong> — a physical unit you tested and accepted, referenced by serial number in the contract.</li>
      <li><strong>The written parameters</strong> — every spec line, including the CCA test basis, signed.</li>
      <li><strong>Change control</strong> — a rule that any change to material, alloy or process requires your written approval.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Without a lock, a repeat order becomes a new development project with fresh approval risk. The <a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-development-process/">development process</a> page shows where each gate must be signed before the lock is released to production.</p>

    <h2>Production and quality: what the factory controls</h2>
    <p>Once the sample is approved, the specification goes to the line. {MANUFACTURER.name} runs {MANUFACTURER.productionLines} with [IATF 16949](https://www.iatfglobaloversight.org/) process control and 100% end-of-line testing — the automotive quality standard that makes container-to-container consistency possible, not just hoped for. What to look for on the line is covered in the <a href="/knowledge/battery-quality-control/">quality control</a> page, and the economic reason lead-acid is worth sourcing this way — near-total recyclability and a dominant-material cost base — is documented by [Battery Council International](https://batterycouncil.org/sources).</p>

    <h2>Repeat orders: the payoff</h2>
    <p>The first order carries the development time — specification, sampling and approval. Repeat orders collapse to production plus shipping ({TRADE_TERMS.leadTime}), because the specification is already locked. That is the economics of the program: the setup cost is amortised across every subsequent container, and the consistency risk is retired by the lock. This is why a program beats a string of one-off purchases.</p>

    <h2>What can go wrong, and where</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Skipped sampling</strong> — approving a drawing but not the physical sample is the single most common failure, and it surfaces as a repeat order that differs from the first.</li>
      <li><strong>Unsigned CCA basis</strong> — if the test method (JIS −15°C vs EN −18°C) is not written down, a later batch can "meet spec" on a different, easier test.</li>
      <li><strong>No change control</strong> — a factory that switches alloy or separator to save cost has no obligation to tell you unless the contract says so.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Where this fits</h2>
    <p>This page covers the program structure. The surrounding pages cover each piece in depth:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-guide/">OEM battery manufacturing: the complete guide</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/what-is-oem-battery/">What is an OEM battery</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-development-process/">The OEM development process</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-cost-breakdown/">OEM battery cost breakdown</a></li>
      <li><a href="/knowledge/oem-battery-vs-aftermarket/">OEM vs aftermarket batteries</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p>{RELATIONSHIP}</p>
    <p><a href="/oem/">Start your OEM battery program &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <p><strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the core of an OEM program is not the first order — it is the specification lock that makes the tenth container match the first approved sample.</p>
      <p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>The lock has three parts: the approved sample, the written parameters including the CCA basis, and change control requiring your written approval.</li>
        <li>Without a lock, a repeat order becomes a new development project with fresh approval risk — which is what a loose one-off order is.</li>
        <li>Repeat orders collapse to production plus shipping, which is where the setup cost amortises and the program actually pays.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><em>Disclosure: this is the author's editorial judgment and field experience, not a verified technical claim.</em></p>
    </div>
  </section>

